commentary.text:
This rule sets out the process of calculations in relation to foreclosure actions, assessments of costs and corrections. Particularly, rule 9.35(4) permits the plaintiff to reappear and seek to vary the decision of an assessment officer and their assessment of costs.
Review of Assessment of Costs – Rule 9.35(4) allows a plaintiff to question an assessment officer’s assessment in a foreclosure action to the applications judge or judge who granted the order. To be open for review, “re-attendance [must] take place before the Order is entered.” Caplink Mortgage Investors Corp. v. Kretschmer, [2012] A.J. No. 637, 2012 ABQB 396 at para. 1 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)).
As such, orders entered to facilitate the closing of a sale to a third party purchaser will bar the application of rule 9.35(4). Caplink Mortgage Investors Corp. v. Kretschmer, [2012] A.J. No. 637, 2012 ABQB 396 at para. 1 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)).
“[T]he determination of the assessment officer, together with the subsequent filing of the Bill of Costs, constitutes the ‘order’ referred to in Rule 9.35(4)”. Equitable Bank v. Avison, [2015] A.J. No. 166, 2015 ABQB 109 at para. 40 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)).
Notice of a rule 9.35(4) application should be provided to the defendant if the defendant appeared at the application where the costs order was granted. Equitable Bank v. Avison, [2015] A.J. No. 166, 2015 ABQB 109 at para. 23 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)).
Rule 9.35(4) is silent about the nature of the process. Applications judges are not supposed to hear appeals (Court of King’s Bench Act, RSA 2000, c. C-31, section 9(1)(a)(i)). Paradigm Quest Inc. v. Moser, [2014] A.J. No. 1375, 2014 ABQB 747 at para. 9 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)), revd on other grounds [2015] A.J. No. 1138, 2015 ABQB 557 (Alta. Q.B.).
Accordingly, applications judges should treat rule 9.35(4) as a application and de novo substitute their discretion for the opinion of the assessment officer, if necessary. Paradigm Quest Inc. v. Moser, [2014] A.J. No. 1375, 2014 ABQB 747 at para. 9 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)), revd on other grounds [2015] A.J. No. 1138, 2015 ABQB 557 (Alta. Q.B.).
An appeal from the review officer’s decision is brought before a judge under rule 10.26.
Assessment of Costs – The following considerations should be applied to a costs determination under rule 9.35(4):
(1) The court is responsible for settling the costs, if this is requested, or mandated by the Rules or the Order;
(2) A contract provision does not oust the jurisdiction of the court;
(3) The court must act in a principled manner in assessing costs;
(4) Full indemnification is unlikely and exceptional, except when the contract specifies “full indemnity costs”. In general terms, the court must enforce a fee agreement unless there is a compelling reason not to do so. The court may choose to hold the party to its bargain;
(5) Steps that advance the purpose of the action merit indemnity;
(6) Unnecessary steps will not be compensated.
(7) [T]he court has an overriding obligation to ensure that those who are obliged to pay for legal services are treated reasonably, taking into account all the PART 9 [2020] A.J. No. 550, 2020 ABQB 294 (Alta. Q.B.). Note that the discussion in focuses on fee De Lara claims.

rules.text:
In this Division, (a) “Act” means Part 3 of the International Conventions Implementation Act and includes the Convention;
(b) “certified copy” means the original document or a copy of the document certified as being a true copy by the original or facsimile signature of a proper officer of the foreign court;
(c) “Convention” means the Convention Between Canada and the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland Providing for the Reciprocal Recognition and Enforcement of Judgments in Civil and Commercial Matters in Schedule 3 to the Act;
(d) “convention judgment”, “convention judgment creditor”, “conven- tion judgment debtor” and “original court” have the same meanings respectively as “judgment”, “judgment creditor”, “judgment debtor” and “original court” have in the Convention.

*[Alta. Reg. 124/2010, r. 9.40 effective November 1, 2010 (Alta. Gaz. August 14, 2010)]*
